Q: LaTex interpreter in Octave fails a &#39;run-time test&#39; (again!) A: <p>Infor for others: it is not direct solution but it helped OP to find real problem.</p>
<hr />
<p>In script you can use <code>getenv()</code> tocheck what values have variables<br />
(without <code>;</code> it will display it in console)</p>
<pre><code>getenv(&quot;OCTAVE_LATEX_BINARY&quot;)
getenv(&quot;OCTAVE_DVIPNG_BINARY&quot;)
getenv(&quot;OCTAVE_DVISVG_BINARY&quot;)
</code></pre>
<p>In script you may also set these values if they are wrong:</p>
<pre><code>setenv(&quot;OCTAVE_LATEX_BINARY&quot;, &quot;latex&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>(Original code works for me without problems but I can get your error when I set here wrong value: e.g <code>setenv(&quot;OCTAVE_LATEX_BINARY&quot;, &quot;hello_world&quot;);</code>)</p>
<p>You can add debug flag to get more details</p>
<pre><code>setenv(&quot;OCTAVE_LATEX_DEBUG_FLAG&quot;, &quot;1&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>and this may show if <code>octave</code> has problem to access <code>latex</code>,<br />
or if <code>latex</code> has problems to access some libraries.</p>
<p>Example output for <code>hello_world</code></p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>sh: 1: hello_world: not found
warning: latex_renderer: latex failed for string &quot;?&quot;
* Command:
        &quot;hello_world&quot; -interaction=nonstopmode -output-directory=&quot;/tmp/latexrShryi&quot; &quot;/tmp/latexrShryi/default.tex&quot;

* Error:


* Stdout:

</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Full code used for test:</p>
<pre class="lang-matlab prettyprint-override"><code>setenv(&quot;OCTAVE_LATEX_DEBUG_FLAG&quot;, &quot;1&quot;)

# setenv(&quot;OCTAVE_LATEX_BINARY&quot;, &quot;hello_world&quot;);

disp([&quot;OCTAVE_LATEX_BINARY : &quot;, getenv(&quot;OCTAVE_LATEX_BINARY&quot;)]);
disp([&quot;OCTAVE_DVIPNG_BINARY: &quot;, getenv(&quot;OCTAVE_DVIPNG_BINARY&quot;)]);
disp([&quot;OCTAVE_DVISVG_BINARY: &quot;, getenv(&quot;OCTAVE_DVISVG_BINARY&quot;)]);

figure();

x = linspace(0, 2*pi, 100);
y = sin(x);
plot(x, y);

xlabel('$x$', 'interpreter', 'latex');

print -dpdf &quot;output.pdf&quot;
print -dpng &quot;output.png&quot;
</code></pre>

